                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2I
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E8"} E8
          %p The correspondence may be understood at different levels:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %strong McKay quivers.
                The original correspondence due to
                %a{:href => "#McKay80"} McKay 80
                is the observation that the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/McKay+quiver"} McKay quiver
                associated to the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbifold"} orbifold
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singularity"} singularity
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℂ
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ⫽
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}^2 \sslash G
                of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+subgroup+of+SU%282%29"} finite subgroup of SU(2)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                      %mo ⊂
                      %mi SU
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G \subset SU(2)
                happens to be an extended
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dynkin+quiver"} Dynkin quiver
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dynkin+diagram"} Dynkin diagram
                of the kind that also arises in the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ADE-classification"} ADE-classification
                of
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simple+Lie+groups"} simple Lie groups
              %p
                More on this in
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a{:href => "#McKayQuivers"} McKay quivers
            %li
              %p
                %strong Equivariant K-theory.
                A more conceptual explanation for this correspondence comes from the classical fact that the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/blow+up"} blow up
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi ℂ
                            %mn 2
                          %mo ⫽
                          %mi G
                        %mo ^
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widehat{\mathbb{C}^2\sslash G}
                of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ADE+singularity"} ADE singularity
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℂ
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ⫽
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}^2\sslash G
                is given by a sequence of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spheres"} spheres
                that touch along the vertices of the corresponding
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dynkin+quiver"} Dynkin quiver
                %a{:href => "#GSV83"} Gonzalez-Sprinberg &amp; Verdier 83
                it was shown that the
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi G
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+K-theory"} equivariant K-theory
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi K
                        %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi ℂ
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_G(\mathbb{C}^2)
                of the singularity is isomorphic to the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory"} K-theory
                of the desingularization
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi ℂ
                            %mn 2
                          %mo ⫽
                          %mi G
                        %mo ^
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widehat {\mathbb{C}^2\sslash G}
                such that under this isomorphism the generators of
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi K
                          %mi G
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msup
                          %mi ℂ
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ≃
                        %msub
                          %mi R
                          %mi ℂ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi G
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_G(\mathbb{C}^2) \simeq R_\mathbb{C}(G)
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation+ring"} representation ring
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/irreducible+representations"} irreducible representations
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exceptional+divisors"} exceptional divisors
                of this blowup. This gives a conceptual geometric/cohomological explanation for the identifications observed by
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "#McKay80"} McKay 80
              %p
                More on this in
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#AsAnEquivalenceOfKtheories"} As an equivalence of K-theories
            %li
              %p
                %strong Seiberg-Witten theory.
                Under the interpretation of
                = succeed "-classes" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory"} K-theory
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-brane"} D-brane
                charges, the K-theoretic McKay correspondence of
                %a{:href => "#GSV83"} Gonzalez-Sprinberg &amp; Verdier 83
                identifies
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wrapped+brane"} wrapped brane
                charges of the desingularized space with
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fractional+brane"} fractional brane
                charges at the singularity.
              %p
                This leads to a further (currently non-rigorous) explanation of the McKay correspondence in terms of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS-CFT+duality"} dual
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"} worldvolume
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theories"} quantum field theories
                on these branes, which are
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/N%3D2+D%3D4+super+Yang-Mills+theory"} N=2
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quiver+gauge+theories"} quiver gauge theories
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space"} moduli space
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scalar+fields"} scalar fields
                of these theories has two “branches”, called the
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higgs+branch"} Higgs branch
                and the
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Coulomb+branch"} Coulomb branch
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum+state"} vacuum state
                of the theory is in, it describes the brane as being either on the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ADE+singularity"} ADE singularity
                or on its
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/resolution+of+singularities"} resolution
          %p
            More on this in
            %em
              %a{:href => "#ViaSuperYangMillsTheory"} Via N=2 SYM theory
            below.
          %h3#McKayQuivers Via McKay quivers
          %p
            Generally, for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+group"} finite group
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+representation"} linear representation
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+dimensional+vector+space"} finite dimensional
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+vector+space"} complex vector space
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/McKay+quiver"} McKay quiver
            or
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/McKay+graph"} McKay graph
            associated with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quiver"} quiver
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertices"} vertices
            correspond to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/irreducible+representations"} irreducible representations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ρ
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho_i
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            and which has
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_{i j} \in \mathbb{N}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/edges"} edges
            between the
            = succeed "th" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            = succeed "th" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_{i j}
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficients"} coefficients
            in the expansion into
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/irreps"} irreps
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+representations"} tensor product of representations
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            with these irreps:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msub
                    %mi ρ
                    %mi i
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≃
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⨁
                    %mi j
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                  %mo ⋅
                  %msub
                    %mi ρ
                    %mi j
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  V \otimes \rho_i
                  \\;\simeq\;
                  \\underset{j}{\bigoplus}
                  a_{i j} \cdot \rho_j
                  \\,.
          %p
            Specifically this applies to the special case where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo ⊂
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G \subset
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SU%282%29"} SU(2)
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+subgroup+of+SU%282%29"} finite subgroup of SU(2)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            its defining representation on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}^2
          %p
            The original
            %em McKay correspondence
            (
            %a{:href => "#McKay80"}> McKay 80
            ) states that in this case the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/McKay+quivers"} McKay quivers
            are
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dynkin+quivers"} Dynkin quivers
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dynkin+diagrams"} Dynkin diagrams
            in the same
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ADE+classification"} ADE classification
            as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ADE+singularity"} ADE singularity
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ⫽
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}^2 \sslash G
          %p
            More precisely: If one uses
            %em all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/irreducible+representations"} irreducible representations
            including the 1-dimensiona
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trivial+representation"} trivial representation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ρ
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho_0
            then one gets the “extended Dynkin diagram”, where the extra node corresponds to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ρ
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho_0
          %div{:style => "text-align: center"}
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/ExtendedADEQuivers.jpg", :width => "400"}/
          %blockquote
            %p
              graphics grabbed from
              %a{:href => "#GSV83"} GSV 83, p. 4
          %p
            In particular, for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mi N
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi SU
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G =\mathbb{Z}_N \subset SU(2)
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cyclic+group"} cyclic group
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/order+of+a+group"} order
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            complex
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/irreps"} irreps
            and the McKay quiver, i.e. the extended Dynkin diagram, has
            = succeed "-vertices," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
          %h3#AsAnEquivalenceOfKtheories As an equivalence of K-theories
          %p
            A strong version of the McKay correspondence is obtained when the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+theory"} cohomology theory
            is taken be (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+K-theory"}> equivariant
            )
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory"} K-theory
            (
            %a{:href => "#GSV83"}> Gonzalez-Sprinberg &amp; Verdier 83
            ):
          %p
            Here the McKay correspondence becomes an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            between the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+K-theory"} equivariant K-theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi G
                        %mi ADE
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_{G_{ADE}}(\ast)
            of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ADE-singularity"} ADE-singularity
            (equivalently the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation+ring"} representation ring
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi G
                      %mi ADE
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R(G_{ADE})
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory"} K-theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(\tilde X)
            of its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/blow-up"} blow-up
            %a{:href => "ADE+singularity#ResolutionBySpheresTouchingAlongADynkinDiagram"} resolution
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde X
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+T-duality"} topological T-duality
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+transform"} integral transform
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fourier-Mukai+transform"}> Fourier-Mukai transform
            ) through the canonical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correspondence"} correspondence
            that these spaces constitute:
          #eq:KMcKay.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi K
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi X
                          %msub
                            %mo ×
                            %mrow
                              %mi X
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                              %mi G
                          %mover
                            %mi X
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mrow
                            %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %msubsup
                                  %mi p
                                  %mn 1
                                  %mo *
                          %mo ↗
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo ↘
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %mi Inv
                                %mo ∘
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %msub
                                  %mi p
                                  %mn 2
                                %msub
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                  %mo *
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi R
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi G
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ≃
                          %msub
                            %mi K
                            %mi G
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mo *
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo ≃
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi K
                            %mi G
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi X
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ⟶
                            %mo ≃
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi K
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mover
                            %mi X
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp;
                  \&amp;&amp; K( X \times_{X/G} \tilde X )
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp;
                  \&amp; {}^{\mathllap{ p_1^\ast }}\nearrow &amp;&amp; \searrow^{\mathrlap{Inv \circ (p_2)_\ast}}
                  \\\
                  R(G) \simeq K_G(\ast) &amp;\simeq&amp; K_G(X) &amp;&amp; \overset{\simeq}{\longrightarrow}  &amp;&amp; K(\tilde X)
                  }
          %p
            In terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"}> string theory
            ), the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory"} K-theory
            classes appearing here may be interpreted as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groups"} groups
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fractional+D-brane"} fractional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-brane+charges"} D-brane charges
            (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "fractional+D-brane#DBranesAtOrbifoldSingularities"} there
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"} worldvolume
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chan-Paton+gauge+field"} Chan-Paton
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            on the D-branes the McKay correspondence is then seen as passage from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higgs+branch"} Higgs branch
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Coulomb+branch"} Coulomb branch
            (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "fractional+D-brane#InTermsOfTheWorldvolumeGaugeTheories"} there
          %p
            The proof of these statements generally proceeds by relating both sides of the equivalence to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dynkin+diagrams"} Dynkin diagrams
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ADE-classification"} ADE-type
          %p
            The classical McKay correspondence, named after
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+McKay"} John McKay
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/McKay+graphs"} McKay graphs
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+group"} finite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subgroups"} subgroups
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo ⊂
                  %msub
                    %mtext SL
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G \subset \text{SL}_2(\mathbb{C})
            and the extended Dynkin diagrams of ADE type.
          %h3#ViaSuperYangMillsTheory
            Via
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                  %mo =
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N=2
            super Yang-Mills theory
          %p
            Various seemingly unrelated structures in mathematics fall into an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ADE+classification"} ADE classification
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+group"} finite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subgroups"} subgroups
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/special+unitary+group"} SU(2)
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+Lie+group"} compact
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simple+Lie+groups"} simple Lie groups
            do. The way this works usually is that one tries to classify these structures somehow, and ends up finding that the classification is governed by the combinatorics of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dynkin+diagrams"} Dynkin diagrams
          %p
            While that does explain a bit, it seems the statement that both the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/icosahedral+group"} icosahedral group
            and the Lie group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E8"} E8
            are related to the same
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dynkin+diagram"} Dynkin diagram
            somehow is still more a question than an answer. Why is that so?
          %p
            The first key insight is due to
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "ADE+singularity#Kronheimer89a"} Kronheimer 89
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbifold"} orbifold
            quotients
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}^2/\Gamma
            for finite subgroups
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SU
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SU(2)
            are precisely the generic form of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+group"} gauge
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbits"} orbits
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+product+group"} direct product group
            of
            = succeed "s" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi n
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(n_i)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sum"} direct sum
            of
            = succeed "-s," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi n
                          %mi i
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi n
                          %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom(\mathbb{C}^{n_i}, \mathbb{C}^{n_j})
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi j
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
            range over the vertices of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dynkin+diagram"} Dynkin diagram
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mo ,
                  %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (i,j)
            over its edges.
          %p
            This becomes more illuminating when interpreted in terms of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quiver+gauge+theory"} quiver gauge theory
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+group"} gauge group
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+product+group"} direct product group
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi n
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(n_i)
            factors associated with vertices of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quiver"} quiver
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particles"} particles
            which are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/charged+particle"} charged
            under this gauge group arrange, as a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+representation"} linear representation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sum"} direct sum
            of
            = succeed "-s," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi n
                          %mi i
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi n
                          %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom(\mathbb{C}^{n_i}, \mathbb{C}^{n_j})
          %p
            Pick one such particle, and follow it around as the gauge group transforms it. The space swept out is its gauge
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbit"} orbit
            %a{:href => "#Kronheimer89"} Kronheimer 89
            says that if the quiver is a Dynkin diagram, then this gauge orbit looks like
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}^2/\Gamma
          %p
            On the other extreme, gauge theories are of interest whose gauge group is not a big direct product, but is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simple+Lie+group"} simple Lie group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/special+unitary+group"} SU(N)
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E8"} E8
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spontaneous+symmetry+breaking"} spontaneous symmetry breaking
            (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higgs+field"}> Higgsing
            ”): the ground state energy of the field theory may happen to be achieved by putting the fields at any one point in a higher dimensional space of field configurations, acted on by the gauge group, and fixing any one such point “spontaneously” singles out the corresponding
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stabilizer+subgroup"} stabilizer subgroup
          %p
            Now here is the final ingredient:
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/N%3D2+D%3D4+super+Yang-Mills+theory"} N=2 D=4 super Yang-Mills theory
            (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Seiberg-Witten+theory"}> Seiberg-Witten theory
            ”). These theories have a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/potential+energy"} potential
            such that its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacua"} vacua
            break a simple gauge group, such as
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi SU
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SU(N)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dynkin+diagram"} Dynkin diagram
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quiver+gauge+theory"} quiver gauge theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "N=2+D=4+super+Yang-Mills+theory#Albertsson03"} Albertsson 03, section 2.3.4
          %p
            More precisely, these theories have two different kinds of vacua, those on the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Coulomb+branch"}> Coulomb branch
            ” and those on the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higgs+branch"}> Higgs branch
            ” depending on whether the scalars of the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+multiplets"}> vector multiplets
            ” (the gauge field sector) or of the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hypermultiplet"}> hypermultiplet
            ” (the matter field sector) vanish. The statement above is for the Higgs branch, but the Coulomb branch is supposed to behave “dually”.
          %p
            So that then finally is the relation, in the ADE classification, between the simple Lie groups and the finite subgroups of SU(2): start with an N=2 super Yang Mills theory with gauge group a simple Lie group. Let it spontaneously find its vacuum and consider the orbit space of the remaining spontaneously broken symmetry group. That is (a resolution of) the orbifold quotient of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}^2
            by a discrete subgroup of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi SU
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SU(2)
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/McKay+quiver"} McKay quiver
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ADE+classification"} ADE classification
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quiver+gauge+theory"} quiver gauge theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wrapped+brane"} wrapped brane
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AGT+correspondence"} AGT correspondence
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3d-3d+correspondence"} 3d-3d correspondence
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/knots-quivers+correspondence"} knots-quivers correspondence
          %h2#references References
          %p The original article is
          %ul
            %li#McKay80
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+McKay"}> John McKay
              ,
              %em Graphs, singularities, and finite groups
              Proc. Symp. Pure Math. Vol. 37. No. 183. 1980
          %p
            As an isomorphism between the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+K-theory"} equivariant K-theory
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ADE-singularity"} ADE-singularity
            and the plain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+K-theory"} topological K-theory
            of its
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "ADE+singularity#ResolutionBySpheresTouchingAlongADynkinDiagram"} resolution
          %ul
            %li#GSV83
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G%C3%A9rard+Gonzalez-Sprinberg"}> Gérard Gonzalez-Sprinberg
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-Louis+Verdier"} Jean-Louis Verdier
              = succeed "," do
                %em Construction géométrique de la correspondance de McKay
              %strong 16
              3 (1983) 409–449 (
              %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item?id=ASENS_1983_4_16_3_409_0"}> numdam:ASENS_1983_4_16_3_409_0
              )
          %p
            An analogous discussion for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+categories"} derived categories
            of coherent sheaves is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tom+Bridgeland"}> Tom Bridgeland
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alastair+King"} Alastair King
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Miles+Reid"} Miles Reid
              = succeed "," do
                %em The McKay correspondence as an equivalence of derived categories
              %a{:href => "https://www.ams.org/journals/jams/2001-14-03/S0894-0347-01-00368-X/S0894-0347-01-00368-X.pdf"} pdf
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1090/S0894-0347-01-00368-X"} doi
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Duiliu-Emanuel+Diaconescu"}> Duiliu-Emanuel Diaconescu
                , Mauro Porta, Francesco Sala,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em McKay correspondence, cohomological Hall algebras and categorification
                %strong 27
                (2023) 933-972 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.13685"}> arXiv:2004.13685
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1090/ert/649"} doi:10.1090/ert/649
            %li
              %p
                J. Denef, F. Loeser,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Motivic integration, quotient singularities and the McKay correspondence
            %li
              %p
                V. Batyrev, D. Dais,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Strong McKay correspondence, string- theoretic Hodge numbers and mirror symmetry
                %strong 35
                (1996) 901–929 alg-geom/9410001
          %p Introductions and surveys include
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Graham Leuschke,
                %em The McKay correspondence
                (
                %a{:href => "http://www.leuschke.org/uploads/McKay-total.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li
              %p
                Bockland,
                %em Character tables and McKay quivers
                (
                %a{:href => "https://staff.fnwi.uva.nl/r.r.j.bocklandt/notes/kleinian.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li
              %p
                Drew Armstrong,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Lectures on the McKay correspondence
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.math.miami.edu/~armstrong/Talks/McKay_Talca.pdf"} pdf scan of notes
            %li
              %p
                Max Lindh,
                %em An Introduction to the McKay Correspondence Master Thesis in Physics
                (
                %a{:href => "http://www.diva-portal.org/smash/get/diva2:1184051/FULLTEXT01.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"}> John Baez
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Geometric McKay Correspondence
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2017/06/the_geometric_mckay_correspond.html"} Part I
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2017/07/the_geometric_mckay_correspond_1.html"} Part II
          %p Literature collection:
          %ul
